# generic derive helpers over a record's fields
#
# these are macro-free, generic functions that walk a record type's fields at
# comptime with `$each f in $fields(T)` and act on each field through the
# projection `v.[f]`. one definition serves every record shape: the `$each`
# unroll is deferred to instantiation, so `T` is a concrete record by the time a
# field descriptor's type gates fold.
#
# the walk descends. a field whose own type is a record is walked in turn, so a
# derive over `Player { pos: Vec3 { x, y, z } }` reaches the three floats rather
# than refusing at `pos`, at any nesting depth.
#
# ## the classification contract
#
# every derive here classifies each field the same way, and `check[T]` is that
# classification written once. a derive's own walk carries only its leaf action
# and relies on `check[T]` for every refusal, which is why each walk's trailing
# `$or { }` is empty rather than defensive: a field that reaches it has already
# failed the build. `check[T]` recurses alongside the walk it guards, so a nested
# record is classified at every depth.
#
# | field shape        | gate                | what happens                     |
# |--------------------|---------------------|----------------------------------|
# | union              | `$is_union`         | refused, with a written message  |
# | reference          | `$is_pointer`       | detected, never followed         |
# | record             | `$is_record`        | descended into                   |
# | scalar numeric     | type comparison     | the derive's leaf action         |
# | anything else      | the `$or` fallback  | refused, with a written message  |
#
# a scalar numeric is a signed or unsigned integer of any width, or a float. a
# `def` over such a scalar counts (its underlying type is what the gate sees), so
# `bool` and `char` fields qualify. the fallback catches arrays, vectors, and
# `^` secrets.
#
# adding a derive is the drop-in this shape is for: write the walk with your own
# leaf action, call `check[T]` first, and every refusal and message comes with
# it. the leaf action is free to allocate or to fail -- `fmt` already
# returns a `res` and returns early from any depth -- so a fallible or
# allocating member takes its extra parameters and its return type as it needs
# them, without the contract changing.
#
# ## the descent is recursion, and it terminates structurally
#
# each derive walks one level and re-enters itself at a record field's own type
# (`eq[f.type](?a.[f], ?b.[f])`). there is no depth limit and none is needed: a
# descent instantiates at a field's type, a record's fields are finite, and a
# record cannot contain itself by value -- the compiler refuses that as a
# recursive type of infinite size. a self-reference has to go through a pointer,
# which is a different type and which `$is_record` does not select.
#
# this replaces a hand-written ladder four levels deep. that bound was never a
# design choice, only what could be spelled: mach could not re-enter a generic at
# a field's type until briar-systems/mach#2691.
#
# the property does NOT extend to following references. `rec Grow[T] { p:
# *Grow[*T]; }` is legal and has unboundedly many distinct instances reachable
# through its pointer, so a reference-following derive needs a termination story
# this one gets for free. see briar-systems/mach#2693.
#
# ## secrets are refused, not skipped
#
# none of these helpers is secrecy-aware, and none can be: no comptime surface
# reports that a field is secret (briar-systems/mach#2694). a secrecy-aware derive
# -- skip in `fmt`, refuse in `hash`, constant-time compare in `eq` -- waits on
# that.
#
# what makes that safe rather than silent is that **nothing strips `^`**. type
# comparison never did, and since briar-systems/mach#2692 the shape predicates do
# not either, so `^u64` fails `f.type == u64` and `^Rec` fails `$is_record`.
# both fall to the fallback and are refused at comptime, with our message rather
# than a raw intrinsic error.
#
# that is why the leaf is an explicit whitelist and the fallback is an `$error`.
# under the current predicate rule a walk that ACTED on its fallback would
# silently skip a secret instead of refusing it, so the refusal is what makes
# "nothing classifies it" a safe answer rather than a dangerous one. inverting
# either gate would let secrets through, and `fmt` would print them.
#
# ## references are detected, never followed
#
# `$is_pointer` sees a reference, and since briar-systems/mach#2693 (mach 4.15.0)
# `$pointee_of` reaches the pointee's shape, so the walk could follow one. it does
# not, and that is now a semantic choice rather than a toolchain limit: address
# semantics versus deep semantics is the caller's to make, and a deep `clone`
# allocates, which means an allocator argument and a failure mode these signatures
# do not have. a reference field is refused. note that `str` is `def str: *char`,
# so a `str` field is a reference field and is refused with the rest.
#
# ## value semantics
#
# equality and hashing follow IEEE-754 through `!=` and a canonical bit fold:
# `-0.0` and `+0.0` are equal and hash alike, while a field holding NaN makes a
# record never equal to itself (NaN compares unequal to everything).

# the walk re-enters itself at a field's type (`eq[f.type](...)`), a spelling mach
# did not accept before briar-systems/mach#2691. that landed on mach `dev` AFTER
# 4.13.0 was tagged, so 4.13.0 does not carry it and 4.14.0 is the first release
# that can build this module. there is no manifest surface that pins a toolchain
# (`mach.lock` records dependency commits and is generated), so the requirement is
# stated here.
#
# THIS GATE CANNOT FIRE FOR THAT SPELLING. `eq[f.type]` is a PARSE error on an
# older compiler, and parsing precedes comptime evaluation, so an older toolchain
# reports "expected a module alias before `.`" against this file rather than the
# message below. that is not a flaw in the gate, it is the missing capability in
# briar-systems/mach#2714: a manifest is read before any source is parsed, so a
# minimum-toolchain key there would diagnose this cleanly, and mach has none.
#
# the gate is kept because it states the requirement where `mach doc` finds it and
# because it would fire for a future requirement that is not a syntax one. until
# #2714 lands it is not what makes an old toolchain diagnosable -- the CHANGELOG
# and this comment are.
$if ($mach.version.major < 4) {
    $error("std.derive: needs mach 4.14.0 or newer; the walk re-enters itself with `eq[f.type]`, which mach did not accept before briar-systems/mach#2691 -- note 4.13.0 does NOT carry it");
}
$or ($mach.version.major == 4 && $mach.version.minor < 14) {
    $error("std.derive: needs mach 4.14.0 or newer; the walk re-enters itself with `eq[f.type]`, which mach did not accept before briar-systems/mach#2691 -- note 4.13.0 does NOT carry it");
}
$or {}

use std.format;
use io_error: std.io.error;
use std.io.writer;
use std.io.writer.WriteError;
use std.types.bool.bool;
use std.types.bool.false;
use std.types.bool.true;
use std.types.result.res;
use std.types.size.usize;
use std.types.string.str;

# FNV-1a 64-bit offset basis
val FNV_OFFSET: u64 = 14695981039346656037;

# FNV-1a 64-bit prime
val FNV_PRIME: u64 = 1099511628211;

# fold the eight bytes of `word` into an FNV-1a accumulator
# ---
# h:    running hash accumulator
# word: the field value packed into a u64
# ret:  the updated accumulator
fun fold(h: u64, word: u64) u64 {
    var x: u64 = h;
    var w: u64 = word;
    var i: i64 = 0;
    for (i < 8) {
        x = (x ^ (w & 0xff)) * FNV_PRIME;
        w = w >> 8;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    ret x;
}

# refuse every field shape the derives here do not walk
#
# this is the classification contract in one place. each derive calls it before
# its own walk, so a rejected record fails the build with a message written
# against the field shape rather than through whichever intrinsic the leaf action
# happened to reach first. it is `pub` so a hand-written derive can hold itself
# to the same rule.
#
# the body is entirely comptime: every arm is empty or an `$error`, so an
# accepted `T` compiles to an empty function. the release pipeline eliminates the
# call; at `-O0` a derive pays one call into it.
# ---
# [T]: the record type to classify
pub fun check[T]() {
    $each f in $fields(T) {
        $if ($is_union(f.type))   { $error("std.derive: a union field cannot be walked -- $fields refuses a union, since its variants overlap in storage and a member walk would report fields that do not all exist. give the union its own operation and call it beside the derive"); }
        $or ($is_pointer(f.type)) { $error("std.derive: a reference field is detected but not followed -- address semantics versus deep semantics is the caller's choice, and a deep walk allocates, so it needs an allocator and a failure mode this signature does not have. note that str is *char, so a str field lands here"); }
        $or ($is_record(f.type))  { check[f.type](); }
        $or (f.type == i8 || f.type == i16 || f.type == i32 || f.type == i64
            || f.type == u8 || f.type == u16 || f.type == u32 || f.type == u64
            || f.type == f32 || f.type == f64) {}
        $or { $error("std.derive: field type is neither a scalar numeric nor a nested record -- an array, a vector, or a ^ secret needs caller-chosen semantics. a ^ field lands here because nothing strips ^: not type comparison, and since briar-systems/mach#2692 not the shape predicates either (briar-systems/mach#2694)"); }
    }
}

# structural equality over a record's fields
#
# compares `a` and `b` field by field, descending into nested records, and
# returns false at the first mismatch. a record with no fields compares equal.
# every field must classify (see `check`); a union, a reference, an array, or a
# `^` secret is a comptime `$error`.
# ---
# [T]: the record type to compare
# a:   first record
# b:   second record
# ret: true when every field, at every depth, compares equal
pub fun eq[T](a: *T, b: *T) bool {
    check[T]();
    $each f in $fields(T) {
        $if ($is_record(f.type)) {
            if (!eq[f.type](?a.[f], ?b.[f])) { ret false; }
        }
        $or (f.type == i8 || f.type == i16 || f.type == i32 || f.type == i64
            || f.type == u8 || f.type == u16 || f.type == u32 || f.type == u64
            || f.type == f32 || f.type == f64) {
            if (a.[f] != b.[f]) { ret false; }
        }
        $or {}
    }
    ret true;
}

# FNV-1a hash over a record's fields
#
# folds each field's value into a 64-bit accumulator in declaration order,
# descending into nested records so a nested field contributes exactly as a
# flattened one would. two records that compare `eq` hash alike, since both walk
# the same fields in the same order. eligibility matches `eq`.
# ---
# [T]: the record type to hash
# v:   the record to hash
# ret: the 64-bit hash
pub fun hash[T](v: *T) u64 {
    check[T]();
    ret fold_fields[T](FNV_OFFSET, v);
}

# fold `v`'s fields into a running accumulator, descending into nested records
#
# the accumulator is threaded through the descent rather than each nested record
# being hashed on its own and combined, so a nested field contributes exactly the
# bytes a flattened one would, so the digest is unchanged from the bounded walk
# this replaced.
# ---
# [T]: the record type to fold
# h0:  the accumulator to fold into
# v:   the record whose fields are folded
# ret: the accumulator after every field, at every depth
fun fold_fields[T](h0: u64, v: *T) u64 {
    var h: u64 = h0;
    $each f in $fields(T) {
        $if ($is_record(f.type)) { h = fold_fields[f.type](h, ?v.[f]); }
        $or (f.type == i8 || f.type == i16 || f.type == i32 || f.type == i64
            || f.type == u8 || f.type == u16 || f.type == u32 || f.type == u64) {
            h = fold(h, (v.[f])::u64);
        }
        $or (f.type == f64) { h = fold(h, (v.[f] + 0.0):~u64); }
        $or (f.type == f32) { h = fold(h, ((v.[f])::f64 + 0.0):~u64); }
        $or                 {}
    }
    ret h;
}

# copy `src` onto `dst` field by field
#
# the value of this over `dst = src` is the refusal, not the copy: a record whose
# every field classifies has no field a memberwise copy could get wrong, so
# `clone[T]` compiling is the proof that a structural copy is a whole copy.
# a field the copy would only shallowly duplicate -- a reference to owned storage
# -- does not classify, and the build fails rather than handing back a second
# record aliasing the first's memory.
#
# an owning clone, which follows references and therefore allocates and can fail,
# is a separate member. briar-systems/mach#2693 (mach 4.15.0) supplies the
# traversal it needs, so what it still waits on is a signature carrying an
# allocator and an error, not a compiler capability.
# ---
# [T]: the record type to copy
# dst: destination record, overwritten field by field
# src: source record
pub fun clone[T](dst: *T, src: *T) {
    check[T]();
    $each f in $fields(T) {
        $if ($is_record(f.type)) { clone[f.type](?dst.[f], ?src.[f]); }
        $or (f.type == i8 || f.type == i16 || f.type == i32 || f.type == i64
            || f.type == u8 || f.type == u16 || f.type == u32 || f.type == u64
            || f.type == f32 || f.type == f64) {
            dst.[f] = src.[f];
        }
        $or {}
    }
}

# write a nested record field's label, `[, ]name=`
#
# the value that follows is written by `fmt` recursing at the field's type, which
# supplies the `Type{...}` half.
# ---
# w:     target writer
# first: true when no field has been written at this level yet
# name:  the field's name
# ret:   bytes written, or the writer's failure with the persisted prefix
fun write_label(w: *writer.Writer, first: bool, name: *u8) res[usize, WriteError] {
    var total: usize = 0;
    if (!first) {
        val sep: res[usize, WriteError] = format.write_str(w, ", ");
        if (sel sep.err) { ret sep; }
        total = sep.ok;
    }
    val nm: res[usize, WriteError] = format.write_str(w, name::str);
    if (sel nm.err) { ret res[usize, WriteError].err{writer.advance(nm.err, total)}; }
    total = total + nm.ok;
    val eq_: res[usize, WriteError] = format.write_byte(w, '=');
    if (sel eq_.err) { ret res[usize, WriteError].err{writer.advance(eq_.err, total)}; }
    ret res[usize, WriteError].ok{total + eq_.ok};
}

# write a record as `Type{field=value, ...}` to a writer
#
# renders each field as `name=value` in declaration order, separated by `, `,
# delegating value formatting to `format.write_value`. a nested record renders
# as `name=Type{...}`, so `Player{pos=Vec3{x=1, y=2, z=3}, id=7}` is one call.
# the type spelling comes from `$type_name`, which is sema's own diagnostic
# authority, so a printed name and an error's name cannot drift.
#
# the outcome is the writer's: a failure at any depth returns at once and
# carries the bytes this call persisted before it.
#
# **this formatter prints `^` secret fields as ordinary values, and it is not
# able to do otherwise.** what saves it today is that a secret field does not
# classify, so `check[T]` fails the build rather than letting one through -- see
# the module note on secrets. do not read that as a redaction: nothing here
# recognizes a secret, and a secrecy-aware `fmt` waits on
# briar-systems/mach#2694.
#
# eligibility matches `eq` and `hash`.
# ---
# [T]: the record type to render
# w:   target writer
# v:   the record to render
# ret: total bytes written, or the writer's failure with the persisted prefix
pub fun fmt[T](w: *writer.Writer, v: *T) res[usize, WriteError] {
    check[T]();
    var total: usize = 0;

    val name: res[usize, WriteError] = format.write_str(w, $type_name(T));
    if (sel name.err) { ret name; }
    total = total + name.ok;

    val open: res[usize, WriteError] = format.write_byte(w, '{');
    if (sel open.err) { ret res[usize, WriteError].err{writer.advance(open.err, total)}; }
    total = total + open.ok;

    var first: bool = true;
    $each f in $fields(T) {
        $if ($is_record(f.type)) {
            # the label, then the field's own rendering. this call writes
            # `Type{...}`, so the two together give `name=Type{...}`.
            val lab: res[usize, WriteError] = write_label(w, first, f.name);
            if (sel lab.err) { ret res[usize, WriteError].err{writer.advance(lab.err, total)}; }
            total = total + lab.ok;
            first = false;

            val nested: res[usize, WriteError] = fmt[f.type](w, ?v.[f]);
            if (sel nested.err) { ret res[usize, WriteError].err{writer.advance(nested.err, total)}; }
            total = total + nested.ok;
        }
        $or (f.type == i8 || f.type == i16 || f.type == i32 || f.type == i64
            || f.type == u8 || f.type == u16 || f.type == u32 || f.type == u64
            || f.type == f32 || f.type == f64) {
            val label: res[usize, WriteError] = write_label(w, first, f.name);
            if (sel label.err) { ret res[usize, WriteError].err{writer.advance(label.err, total)}; }
            total = total + label.ok;
            first = false;

            val leaf: res[usize, WriteError] = format.write_value(w, v.[f]);
            if (sel leaf.err) { ret res[usize, WriteError].err{writer.advance(leaf.err, total)}; }
            total = total + leaf.ok;
        }
        $or {}
    }

    val close: res[usize, WriteError] = format.write_byte(w, '}');
    if (sel close.err) { ret res[usize, WriteError].err{writer.advance(close.err, total)}; }
    total = total + close.ok;

    ret res[usize, WriteError].ok{total};
}

use std.memory;
use std.types.char.char;
use std.types.string.str_equals;

#[testing]
rec DV2 { x: i64; y: i64; }
#[testing]
rec DMix { i: i32; u: u16; f: f64; g: f32; }
#[testing]
rec DFlags { on: bool; c: char; n: u8; }

#[testing]
rec DEmpty {}

rec NEmpty {}

#[testing]
rec NHolder { e: NEmpty; z: i64; }

# six levels deep, so a walk that stops short of a leaf is caught
rec D6 { a: i64; b: f64; }
rec D5 { d: D6; k: u16; }
rec D4 { d: D5; k: u16; }
rec D3 { d: D4; k: u16; }
rec D2 { d: D3; n: i32; }
rec D1 { d: D2; tag: u8; }

#[testing]
fun d1_fill(v: *D1) {
    v.d.d.d.d.d.a = 11;
    v.d.d.d.d.d.b = 2.5;
    v.d.d.d.d.k   = 22;
    v.d.d.d.k     = 33;
    v.d.d.k       = 44;
    v.d.n         = -55;
    v.tag         = 66;
}

# a copy of `v` with the leaf numbered `leaf` changed, leaves counted from the
# innermost; 7 or more changes nothing
#[testing]
fun d1_perturbed(v: *D1, leaf: i32) D1 {
    var b: D1 = @v;
    if (leaf == 0) { b.d.d.d.d.d.a = b.d.d.d.d.d.a + 1; }
    or (leaf == 1) { b.d.d.d.d.d.b = b.d.d.d.d.d.b + 0.25; }
    or (leaf == 2) { b.d.d.d.d.k = b.d.d.d.d.k + 1; }
    or (leaf == 3) { b.d.d.d.k = b.d.d.d.k + 1; }
    or (leaf == 4) { b.d.d.k = b.d.d.k + 1; }
    or (leaf == 5) { b.d.n = b.d.n + 1; }
    or (leaf == 6) { b.tag = b.tag + 1; }
    ret b;
}

# scalar, float, def-typed and empty records; signed zero is equal and NaN is
# never equal, as the field's own == says
test eq__flat_records {
    var a: DMix = DMix{i: -2, u: 9, f: 1.5, g: 2.5};
    var b: DMix = DMix{i: -2, u: 9, f: 1.5, g: 2.5};
    if (!eq[DMix](?a, ?b)) { ret 1; }
    b.f = 1.6;
    if (eq[DMix](?a, ?b)) { ret 2; }
    b.f = 1.5;
    b.i = -3;
    if (eq[DMix](?a, ?b)) { ret 3; }

    var fa: DFlags = DFlags{on: true, c: 'z', n: 5};
    var fb: DFlags = DFlags{on: true, c: 'y', n: 5};
    if (eq[DFlags](?fa, ?fb)) { ret 4; }

    var ea: DEmpty;
    var eb: DEmpty;
    if (!eq[DEmpty](?ea, ?eb)) { ret 5; }

    var negz: u64  = 0x8000000000000000;
    var z:    DMix = DMix{i: 0, u: 0, f: 0.0, g: 0.0};
    var nz:   DMix = z;
    memory.raw_copy(?nz.f, ?negz, 8);
    if (!eq[DMix](?z, ?nz)) { ret 6; }
    var nan: u64 = 0x7FF8000000000000;
    memory.raw_copy(?nz.f, ?nan, 8);
    if (eq[DMix](?nz, ?nz)) { ret 7; }
    ret 0;
}

# a difference in any leaf at any depth is seen, and an empty nested record
# does not decide equality
test eq__a_difference_at_every_depth_is_seen {
    var a: D1;
    d1_fill(?a);
    var leaf: i32 = 0;
    for (leaf < 8) {
        var b:    D1   = d1_perturbed(?a, leaf);
        val want: bool = leaf == 7;
        if (eq[D1](?a, ?b) != want) { ret leaf + 1; }
        leaf = leaf + 1;
    }

    var ha: NHolder;
    ha.z = 1;
    var hb: NHolder;
    hb.z = 2;
    if (eq[NHolder](?ha, ?hb)) { ret 10; }
    hb.z = 1;
    if (!eq[NHolder](?ha, ?hb)) { ret 11; }
    ret 0;
}

# records that compare eq hash alike (signed zero included), and a difference
# in any leaf at any depth, or in field order, changes the hash
test hash__follows_eq_at_every_depth {
    var a: D1;
    d1_fill(?a);
    var leaf: i32 = 0;
    for (leaf < 8) {
        var b:    D1   = d1_perturbed(?a, leaf);
        val same: bool = hash[D1](?a) == hash[D1](?b);
        if (same != (leaf == 7)) { ret leaf + 1; }
        leaf = leaf + 1;
    }

    var xy: DV2 = DV2{x: 1, y: 2};
    var yx: DV2 = DV2{x: 2, y: 1};
    if (hash[DV2](?xy) == hash[DV2](?yx)) { ret 10; }

    var negz: u64  = 0x8000000000000000;
    var z:    DMix = DMix{i: 0, u: 0, f: 0.0, g: 0.0};
    var nz:   DMix = z;
    memory.raw_copy(?nz.f, ?negz, 8);
    if (hash[DMix](?z) != hash[DMix](?nz)) { ret 11; }

    var fa: DFlags = DFlags{on: true, c: 'q', n: 200};
    var fb: DFlags = DFlags{on: true, c: 'q', n: 200};
    if (hash[DFlags](?fa) != hash[DFlags](?fb)) { ret 12; }
    ret 0;
}

rec DCap { data: *u8; len: usize; cap: usize; }

#[testing]
fun cap_write(ctx: ptr, p: *u8, len: usize) res[usize, WriteError] {
    val b: *DCap = ctx::*DCap;
    var i: usize = 0;
    for (i < len) {
        if (b.len >= b.cap) {
            ret res[usize, WriteError].err{writer.native_failure(i, io_error.make(io_error.RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED, io_error.OP_WRITE))};
        }
        b.data[b.len] = p[i];
        b.len         = b.len + 1;
        i             = i + 1;
    }
    ret res[usize, WriteError].ok{len};
}

#[testing]
fun cap_writer(cap: *DCap, data: *u8, size: usize) writer.Writer {
    cap.data = data;
    cap.len  = 0;
    cap.cap  = size;
    var w: writer.Writer;
    w.ctx     = cap::ptr;
    w.f_write = cap_write;
    ret w;
}

test fmt__flat_and_empty_records {
    var scratch: [128]u8;
    var cap:     DCap;
    var w:       writer.Writer = cap_writer(?cap, ?scratch[0], 128);

    var v: DV2                    = DV2{x: 1, y: 2};
    val r: res[usize, WriteError] = fmt[DV2](?w, ?v);
    if (sel r.err) { ret 1; }
    scratch[cap.len] = 0;
    if (!str_equals(?scratch[0], "DV2{x=1, y=2}")) { ret 2; }

    w = cap_writer(?cap, ?scratch[0], 128);
    var h: NHolder;
    h.z = 9;
    val rh: res[usize, WriteError] = fmt[NHolder](?w, ?h);
    if (sel rh.err) { ret 3; }
    scratch[cap.len] = 0;
    if (!str_equals(?scratch[0], "NHolder{e=NEmpty{}, z=9}")) { ret 4; }
    ret 0;
}

test fmt__six_levels_render_every_leaf {
    var scratch: [256]u8;
    var cap:     DCap;
    var w:       writer.Writer = cap_writer(?cap, ?scratch[0], 256);

    var v: D1;
    d1_fill(?v);
    val r: res[usize, WriteError] = fmt[D1](?w, ?v);
    if (sel r.err) { ret 1; }
    scratch[cap.len] = 0;
    if (!str_equals(?scratch[0], "D1{d=D2{d=D3{d=D4{d=D5{d=D6{a=11, b=2.5}, k=22}, k=33}, k=44}, n=-55}, tag=66}")) { ret 2; }
    ret 0;
}

test fmt__a_writer_error_at_depth_propagates {
    var scratch: [16]u8;
    var cap:     DCap;
    var w:       writer.Writer = cap_writer(?cap, ?scratch[0], 8);

    var v: D1;
    d1_fill(?v);
    val r: res[usize, WriteError] = fmt[D1](?w, ?v);
    if (!sel r.err) { ret 1; }
    # the sink took its 8 bytes before refusing, and the error says so
    if (!sel r.err.native)            { ret 2; }
    if (writer.persisted(r.err) != 8) { ret 3; }
    scratch[cap.len] = 0;
    if (!str_equals(?scratch[0], "D1{d=D2{")) { ret 4; }
    ret 0;
}

test clone__every_leaf_copied_and_independent {
    var src: D1;
    d1_fill(?src);
    var dst: D1;
    clone[D1](?dst, ?src);
    # named leaf by leaf, so a walk that drops a level fails here rather than
    # passing on an eq that dropped the same level
    if (dst.d.d.d.d.d.a != 11)  { ret 1; }
    if (dst.d.d.d.d.d.b != 2.5) { ret 2; }
    if (dst.d.d.d.d.k != 22)    { ret 3; }
    if (dst.d.d.d.k != 33)      { ret 4; }
    if (dst.d.d.k != 44)        { ret 5; }
    if (dst.d.n != -55)         { ret 6; }
    if (dst.tag != 66)          { ret 7; }
    src.d.d.d.d.d.a = 99;
    if (dst.d.d.d.d.d.a != 11) { ret 8; }

    var esrc: DEmpty;
    var edst: DEmpty;
    clone[DEmpty](?edst, ?esrc);
    ret 0;
}
